1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,466
Aarav checks the same
resistor with two meters.

2
00:00:03,466 --> 00:00:05,679
The needle meter says four volts.

3
00:00:06,029 --> 00:00:08,918
The digital meter says
four point nine seven.

4
00:00:08,918 --> 00:00:10,798
Same resistor, same moment.

5
00:00:10,798 --> 00:00:12,892
Which one is telling the truth?

6
00:00:13,392 --> 00:00:15,025
Both are multimeters.

7
00:00:15,025 --> 00:00:19,957
One box that measures voltage, current
and resistance, for both AC and

8
00:00:19,957 --> 00:00:20,857
DC.

9
00:00:20,814 --> 00:00:23,866
A rotary switch picks the
quantity and the range.

10
00:00:23,866 --> 00:00:27,699
Two probes plug in, and the
black one always goes to common.

11
00:00:28,349 --> 00:00:32,554
Inside an analogue multimeter
sits a single moving coil meter.

12
00:00:32,904 --> 00:00:37,934
The switch connects shunts to make an
ammeter, multipliers to make a voltmeter,

13
00:00:37,934 --> 00:00:40,854
a rectifier for AC, and a cell for ohms.

14
00:00:41,204 --> 00:00:44,413
You read the answer from the
needle, on the matching scale.

15
00:00:44,763 --> 00:00:48,698
Its sensitivity is one divided
by the full scale current.

16
00:00:48,698 --> 00:00:53,059
A fifty microampere movement gives
twenty thousand ohms per volt.

17
00:00:53,709 --> 00:00:56,419
A digital multimeter
works in a different way.

18
00:00:56,769 --> 00:01:00,973
First, the input stage scales the
signal down to a small voltage.

19
00:01:01,323 --> 00:01:04,745
Converters turn every
quantity into a DC voltage.

20
00:01:04,745 --> 00:01:06,069
AC is rectified.

21
00:01:06,069 --> 00:01:09,212
Current flows through
a small known resistor.

22
00:01:09,212 --> 00:01:12,031
A resistor gets a steady known current.

23
00:01:12,381 --> 00:01:16,404
Then an analogue to digital converter
measures that voltage, and turns

24
00:01:16,404 --> 00:01:17,655
it into a number.

25
00:01:18,005 --> 00:01:22,726
A counter and decoder drive the
display, so the digits appear directly.

26
00:01:22,726 --> 00:01:26,864
Its input resistance is very
high, typically ten million ohms.

27
00:01:27,514 --> 00:01:30,231
Now back to Aarav's
puzzle on the breadboard.

28
00:01:30,581 --> 00:01:34,513
Two resistors of one hundred
kilo ohms each share ten volts.

29
00:01:34,513 --> 00:01:36,684
Each one should get five volts.

30
00:01:37,034 --> 00:01:40,371
Connect the analogue meter
on its ten volt range.

31
00:01:40,371 --> 00:01:45,088
Its two hundred kilo ohms now sits
in parallel with the lower resistor.

32
00:01:45,438 --> 00:01:46,779
Watch the electrons.

33
00:01:46,779 --> 00:01:51,215
Some current leaks through the meter,
and on the scope, the voltage across

34
00:01:51,215 --> 00:01:53,334
the resistor drops to four volts.

35
00:01:53,984 --> 00:01:55,859
Swap in the digital meter.

36
00:01:55,859 --> 00:02:00,387
Ten million ohms draws almost nothing,
and the voltage climbs back close

37
00:02:00,387 --> 00:02:01,287
to five.

38
00:02:01,817 --> 00:02:03,218
Let's check the numbers.

39
00:02:03,568 --> 00:02:08,774
One hundred kilo ohms in parallel with
two hundred kilo ohms is sixty six

40
00:02:08,774 --> 00:02:10,597
point seven kilo ohms.

41
00:02:11,297 --> 00:02:16,181
So the analogue meter reads ten times
sixty six point seven, divided by

42
00:02:16,181 --> 00:02:18,983
one hundred and sixty six point seven.

43
00:02:18,983 --> 00:02:20,553
That is four volts.

44
00:02:21,253 --> 00:02:26,023
With the digital meter, one hundred
kilo ohms in parallel with ten mega

45
00:02:26,023 --> 00:02:28,653
ohms is about ninety nine kilo ohms.

46
00:02:28,653 --> 00:02:32,049
The reading is four point
nine seven five volts.

47
00:02:32,749 --> 00:02:35,087
This error is called the loading effect.

48
00:02:35,087 --> 00:02:38,319
The higher the meter's
resistance, the smaller the error.

49
00:02:38,969 --> 00:02:43,135
A good voltmeter is almost invisible
to the circuit it measures.

50
00:02:43,934 --> 00:02:45,618
So how do the two compare?

51
00:02:45,968 --> 00:02:50,450
An analogue meter shows changes
smoothly as a moving needle, and needs

52
00:02:50,450 --> 00:02:53,189
no battery to measure volts and amperes.

53
00:02:53,539 --> 00:02:57,248
But you must pick the right scale,
and if you look at the needle from an

54
00:02:57,248 --> 00:02:59,067
angle, you get a parallax error.

55
00:02:59,717 --> 00:03:04,158
A digital meter is more accurate, has
a much higher input resistance, and

56
00:03:04,158 --> 00:03:05,857
shows the number directly.

57
00:03:05,857 --> 00:03:08,058
Many even pick the range for you.

58
00:03:08,408 --> 00:03:12,917
But it needs a battery, and a quickly
changing value makes its digits flicker.

59
00:03:13,567 --> 00:03:14,635
One more term.

60
00:03:14,635 --> 00:03:18,787
A three and a half digit display
shows up to one thousand nine hundred

61
00:03:18,787 --> 00:03:19,950
and ninety nine.

62
00:03:19,950 --> 00:03:24,149
The half digit is the leading one,
which can only be zero or one.

63
00:03:24,799 --> 00:03:26,265
Now, the exam trap.

64
00:03:26,615 --> 00:03:30,879
Many students think a voltmeter never
changes the circuit it measures.

65
00:03:31,229 --> 00:03:32,129
It does.

66
00:03:31,914 --> 00:03:35,992
Its resistance sits in parallel with
the part, and pulls the reading down

67
00:03:35,992 --> 00:03:36,892
a little.

68
00:03:36,722 --> 00:03:38,414
That is the loading effect.

69
00:03:39,064 --> 00:03:40,713
Where is this asked?

70
00:03:40,713 --> 00:03:44,963
In PUC board papers, comparing
analogue and digital meters.

71
00:03:44,963 --> 00:03:49,933
And in JEE Main, as numericals on
what a real voltmeter reads across a

72
00:03:49,933 --> 00:03:50,833
resistor.

73
00:03:51,424 --> 00:03:52,324
Try this.

74
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:56,567
Two twenty kilo ohm resistors are
in series across twelve volts.

75
00:03:56,567 --> 00:04:01,553
A voltmeter of resistance twenty kilo
ohms is connected across one of them.

76
00:04:01,553 --> 00:04:03,002
What does it read?

77
00:04:08,402 --> 00:04:09,302
B.

78
00:04:08,939 --> 00:04:10,142
Four volts.

79
00:04:10,492 --> 00:04:14,498
The meter and the resistor in
parallel make ten kilo ohms.

80
00:04:14,848 --> 00:04:20,095
The total is thirty kilo ohms, so the
current is twelve divided by thirty

81
00:04:20,095 --> 00:04:23,159
kilo ohms, zero point four milliamperes.

82
00:04:23,510 --> 00:04:27,951
The reading is zero point four
milliamperes times ten kilo ohms.

83
00:04:27,951 --> 00:04:29,507
That is four volts.

84
00:04:30,157 --> 00:04:31,678
Screenshot this card.

85
00:04:34,879 --> 00:04:37,257
Next, the dual channel oscilloscope.

86
00:04:37,257 --> 00:04:40,159
A meter that draws the
voltage as a picture.
